    "q-72721": {
      "dom_id": "step-q72721",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Suppose $B$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(B)=-21.$ The matrix $C$ is obtained from $B$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{4}$ (switching the first and the fourth rows). What is the value of $det(C)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since swapping any two rows changes the sign of the determinant to the opposite sign, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C) & =-det(B) \\\\  & =-(-21) \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
