      "source_example_id": "e-4423",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating a Determinant After Multiplying a Row by a Number",
      "kp_id": "e-4423"
    },
    "q-72746": {
      "dom_id": "step-q72746",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Suppose $C$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $det(C)=-5.$ What is the value of $det(-4C)?$",
      "worked_solution": "Multiplying a $n\\times n$ matrix by a scalar $k$ is equivalent to multiplying each row by $k,$ and this occurs $n$ times. This leads to the result\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(k\\cdot C)={k}^{n}\\cdot det(C).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $k=-4$ and $n=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(-4C) & =(-4)^{3}\\cdot det(C) \\\\  & =(-64)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =320.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
