    "q-72749": {
      "dom_id": "step-q72749",
      "name": "Question 18",
      "problem": "Suppose $X$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and $det(X)=39.$ The matrix $Y$ is obtained from $X$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{4}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{4}$ and ${R}_{5}:={R}_{5}+(-3){R}_{1}.$ What is the value of $det(Y)?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{5}:={R}_{5}+(-3){R}_{1}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{4}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{4}$ involves multiplying a row by $\\frac{1}{3},$ so the determinant is multiplied by $\\frac{1}{3}$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $\\frac{1}{3}.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(Y) & =\\frac{1}{3}det(X) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 39 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
