    "q-130769": {
      "dom_id": "step-q130769",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "Suppose $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $det(A)=11.$ The matrix $B$ is obtained from $A$ by applying the elementary row operations ${R}_{4}:=3{R}_{4}$ and ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}.$ What is the value of $det(B)?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's figure out what each operation does to the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-5){R}_{1}$ involves adding a multiple of one row to another row,  so it does not change the determinant.\n\n- The operation ${R}_{4}:=3{R}_{4}$ involves multiplying a row by $3,$ so the determinant is multiplied by $3$ as well.\n\nTherefore, we only have to multiply the determinant by $3.$ Consequently, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(B) & =3det(A) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 11 \\\\  & =33.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
